<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Sarangarh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Sarangarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Raj Gond state of the Chhattisgarh plain on the Mahanadi, whose last ruler became Chief Minister of Madhya Pradesh.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sarangarh was ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raj-gond">Raj Gond</a> family, one of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond</a> dynasties of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>. Its traditions date the state to around 1600, and it was long a dependency of larger neighbours, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagpur-kingdom">Bhonsles of Nagpur</a> in the 18th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Raja ruled a modest territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields and forest near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi">Mahanadi</a>. Under British paramountcy Sarangarh was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh-feudatory-states">Chhattisgarh Feudatory States</a>, later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-states-agency">Eastern States Agency</a>. Its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresh-chandra-singh">Naresh Chandra Singh</a>, took an active part in politics.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sarangarh merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces and Berar</a> in 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresh-chandra-singh">Naresh Chandra Singh</a> later served briefly as Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1969. The area is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sarangarh-state.jpg" alt="Sarangarh State" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sakti-Raigarh-Imperial_Gazetteer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>